---
/*
  /lab/fields — a LOCAL-ONLY bench, dev-gated below, linked from nowhere.

  ONE QUESTION, AND IT IS THE ONLY BLOCKING ONE IN PLAN 38: does the Observatory
  have one field grammar or two? Michael, 2026-08-12: *"yeah lets do the lab, and
  I can decide whats best that way."*

  WHY IT NEEDED A BENCH RATHER THAN A PARAGRAPH. The plan can tell you that an HQ
  label is 10px uppercase at 48% and a quote sheet's is 14px sentence-case at
  70%; it cannot tell you which one is right at a desk, and the plan's own hands
  item says so — *"this is the one that could reverse the decision, and it can
  only be answered by looking."* Same reason 36 was decided at /lab/chrome and 33
  at /lab/music rather than on paper.

  ⚠ THE FRAMING IN §0's TABLE IS WRONG AND THIS BENCH IS ARRANGED AROUND THE
  CORRECTION. The plan reads the split as "two stylesheets, `hq.css` vs
  `admin.css`". Measured, the two NAMED primitives are on the same side:

      .f__k          10px   UPPER   .06em   48%    25 uses
      .admin-label   11.2px UPPER   .08em   50%    12 uses
      inline span    14px   sentence  —     70%    14 uses  ← the actual outlier

  So merging `.admin-label` into `.f__k` is a FACT — 1.2px and 2% apart, nobody
  would argue to keep both. The decision is only whether the corpus sheets keep
  the third row. That is ~20 sites, not 60, and it is a question about REGISTER,
  not about which file a rule lives in.

  ⚠ AND ONE VARIANT IS HERE BECAUSE OF CONTRAST, NOT TASTE. Plan 19 derived the
  ink ramp by measurement: dusk crosses 4.5:1 at 55% opacity, paper not until
  65%. `.f__k` sits at 48% and `.f__h` at 42% — BOTH BELOW THE FLOOR THIS REPO
  DERIVED FOR ITSELF — and 19 · Piece 5, the 362-site migration that fixes that,
  is still unbuilt. So "migrate the corpus to `.f`" as written would move 14
  labels that currently PASS onto a primitive that fails, and hand Piece 5 more
  work. `ramp` below is `.f`'s geometry at the floor, and if `.f` wins it should
  win in that costume rather than today's.

  ⚠ ONE SOURCE, FOUR RENDERS — the chrome bench's rule, for its reason. The form
  markup below is written ONCE and mapped over the four registers; every
  difference is CSS keyed off `[data-fx]` on the section. So `hq` is not a copy
  of the shipped `.f` — it IS `.f` with this file's stylesheet doing nothing, and
  there is no drift to police. The whole experiment deletes in one file.

  ⚠ WHAT THIS BENCH DOES NOT DECIDE, deliberately, so the question stays one
  question: not sheet widths, not button hues (`cn-amber` stays amber — a real
  domain axis `admin.md` §1 argues for), not the sheet shell (29 · §6 owns it).
  Label geometry, hint geometry, required marking and input chrome. Nothing else.
*/
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
// The admin's own primitives, exactly as an admin page gets them: `hq.css` from
// the layout, `admin.css` inlined by `app.css` (which `Base` brings). Both sets
// are already loaded on every admin page, which is the finding that makes the
// migration markup-only — no rule moves between files whichever way this goes.
import '../../styles/hq.css';

if (import.meta.env.PROD) return Astro.redirect('/', 302);

/**
 * The four propositions.
 *
 * `hq` and `corpus` are the two that exist in the tree today and are what a
 * migration would choose between. `admin` is here to be DISMISSED on sight —
 * it is the third primitive, and seeing how little separates it from `hq` is
 * most of the argument for deleting it. `ramp` is `hq` at the contrast floor.
 */
const REGISTERS = [
  {
    key: 'hq',
    name: 'HQ — .f',
    note: '10px · UPPERCASE · 48% — 25 uses. Task, Goal, Event, Person, Tag, Link.',
    verdict: 'Ships today in the seven HQ sheets.',
  },
  {
    key: 'admin',
    name: 'Workshop — .admin-label',
    note: '11.2px · UPPERCASE · 50% — 12 uses. The composer, the picker.',
    verdict: 'The third primitive. 1.2px and 2% from the first.',
  },
  {
    key: 'corpus',
    name: 'Corpus sheets — inline',
    note: '14px · sentence case · 70% — 14 uses. Quote, Song, the fragment panel.',
    verdict: 'Never a class. Copied span to span, and about.astro named it.',
  },
  {
    key: 'ramp',
    name: 'HQ at the ink floor',
    note: "10px · UPPERCASE · 80/65% — doesn't exist yet. Plan 19's derived floor.",
    verdict: 'What .f would have to become to pass 4.5:1 in both themes.',
  },
  /* ── ADDED 2026-08-12, AFTER MICHAEL PICKED `admin` ────────────────────────
     *"Workshop — .admin-label definitely the best option here."*

     ⚠ THE PICK AND THE FAILURE ARE ON DIFFERENT AXES, WHICH THE FIRST FOUR
     COLUMNS HID. He chose a REGISTER — 11.2px, uppercase, .08em. What fails
     4.5:1 is the INK — 50% on the label, 45% on the hint. Those are
     independent, and the bench happened to offer its only passing ink attached
     to the OTHER geometry, so "the register I want, legible" was not on screen
     to be chosen.

     This column is that: his pick, unchanged in size, case and tracking, with
     the opacity raised to the floor that passes in both themes. It exists
     because raising ink is not neutral — it makes a label heavier and more
     present, and he chose partly on how quiet it was. If he prefers `admin` at
     50% AFTER seeing this, that is a decision about contrast made with the
     number in view, which is a different thing from one made without it. */
  {
    key: 'adminramp',
    name: 'Workshop at the ink floor',
    note: '11.2px · UPPERCASE · 80/65% — the pick, at a legible ink.',
    verdict: 'Same register. Only the opacity moved.',
  },
] as const;

const MONTHS = ['—', 'January', 'February', 'March'];
---

<Base title="Fields — bench">
  <div class="lab">
    <header class="lab__head">
      <h1>One building, two grammars — the field question</h1>
      <p>
        Plan 38 · §2. The same form in four registers, one source. Judge the <strong>label</strong>, the <strong
          >hint</strong
        >, the <strong>required</strong> mark and the <strong>input chrome</strong> — nothing else on this page is being proposed.
      </p>
      <div class="lab__ctl">
        <button type="button" data-view="grid" aria-pressed="true">Side by side</button>
        <button type="button" data-view="solo" aria-pressed="false">One at a time</button>
        <span class="lab__sep" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <button type="button" data-w="wide" aria-pressed="true">Desk</button>
        <button type="button" data-w="phone" aria-pressed="false">390px</button>
        <span class="lab__sep" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <button type="button" data-theme-flip>Flip theme</button>
      </div>
      <p class="lab__q">
        <b>The question to hold while you look:</b> a field label is scaffolding you read once, and the VALUE is the interesting
        thing — which argues quiet. But some of these labels carry a question you have to think about (“Where in it”, “Known
        since”), and those earn their size. Both kinds are on this page.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="lab__grid">
      {
        REGISTERS.map((r) => (
          <section class="fx" data-fx={r.key}>
            <div class="fx__head">
              <h2>{r.name}</h2>
              <p class="fx__note">{r.note}</p>
              <p class="fx__verdict">{r.verdict}</p>
              {/* Filled by the script — measured in THIS theme, on every flip. */}
              <p class="fx__ratio" data-ratio />
            </div>

            {/* ── THE FORM. Written once; every register renders this exact DOM. ──
                The fields are real ones, taken from both halves on purpose: Name
                and Circle are PersonSheet's, Title and Album are SongSheet's,
                "Where in it" is FragmentSheet's, and the group heading is `.fs__k`
                from the HQ sheets. A register that only looks right on its own
                half is not a candidate. */}
            <form class="fs" onsubmit="return false">
              <p class="fs__k">Who said it</p>

              <label class="f">
                <span class="f__k">
                  Name <em>required</em>
                </span>
                <input type="text" value="Miles Davis" />
              </label>

              <label class="f">
                <span class="f__k">Where in it</span>
                <input type="text" placeholder="ch. 4, or a timestamp" />
                <span class="f__h">Shown after the attribution, in the reader's own line.</span>
              </label>

              <div class="f">
                <span class="f__k">
                  Circle <em>required</em>
                </span>
                <div class="pseg" role="group" aria-label="Circle">
                  <button type="button" class="pseg__b" aria-pressed="true">
                    Inner
                  </button>
                  <button type="button" class="pseg__b" aria-pressed="false">
                    Close
                  </button>
                  <button type="button" class="pseg__b" aria-pressed="false">
                    Wider
                  </button>
                </div>
              </div>

              <label class="f">
                <span class="f__k">Known since</span>
                <select>
                  {MONTHS.map((m) => (
                    <option>{m}</option>
                  ))}
                </select>
              </label>

              <label class="f">
                <span class="f__k">The words themselves</span>
                <textarea rows="3">Sometimes it takes you a long time to sound like yourself.</textarea>
                <span class="f__h">Poetry line breaks survive; the body stores Markdown.</span>
              </label>
            </form>
          </section>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <footer class="lab__foot">
      <p>
        <b>What each answer costs.</b> Picking <code>corpus</code> means the seven HQ sheets move — 25 `.f__k` sites plus
        their hints. Picking <code>hq</code> or <code>ramp</code> means the corpus sheets move — 14 inline spans plus about.astro's
        6. Either way <code>admin</code>'s 12 sites are deleted, because nothing argues for a third. <b
          >Nothing here is a migration yet</b
        >; this page is thrown away either way.
      </p>
    </footer>
  </div>
</Base>

<style is:global>
  /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     THE BENCH'S OWN CHROME. Deliberately plain and deliberately NOT using any
     primitive under test — a lab that dresses itself in the thing it is judging
     is a lab that has already voted.
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
  .lab {
    max-width: 96rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: 2rem 1.5rem 6rem;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
  }
  .lab__head h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.75rem;
  }
  .lab__head > p {
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
    max-width: 60ch;
    color: color-mix(in oklch, var(--color-base-content) 70%, transparent);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }
  .lab__q {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    max-width: 68ch;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--color-primary);
    padding-left: 0.875rem;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .lab__ctl {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }
  .lab__ctl button {
    border: 1px solid var(--color-base-300);
    border-radius: 0.5rem;
    background: var(--color-base-100);
    padding: 0.3rem 0.7rem;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .lab__ctl button[aria-pressed='true'] {
    background: var(--color-primary);
    color: var(--color-primary-content);
    border-color: var(--color-primary);
  }
  .lab__sep {
    width: 1px;
    height: 1.25rem;
    background: var(--color-base-300);
  }
  .lab__grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(19rem, 1fr));
  }
  /* Solo: one column, so a register is judged at a real form's width rather
     than in a 19rem gutter. The grid is for spotting differences; solo is for
     deciding whether one of them is actually good. */
  .lab[data-view='solo'] .lab__grid {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    max-width: 34rem;
  }
  /* 390px, because `.f` is a phone-first grammar and that claim is half of the
     case against converging on it. A register that only works at a desk loses
     here, visibly, rather than in an argument. */
  .lab[data-w='phone'] .fx {
    max-width: 390px;
  }
  .lab[data-w='phone'] .lab__grid {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(390px, 390px));
  }

  .fx {
    border: 1px solid var(--color-base-300);
    border-radius: 0.75rem;
    background: var(--color-base-100);
    padding: 1.25rem;
  }
  .fx__head {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-base-300);
    padding-bottom: 0.75rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }
  .fx__head h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
  }
  .fx__note {
    margin-top: 0.2rem;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    color: color-mix(in oklch, var(--color-base-content) 60%, transparent);
  }
  .fx__verdict {
    margin-top: 0.35rem;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-style: italic;
    color: color-mix(in oklch, var(--color-base-content) 50%, transparent);
  }
  .fx__ratio {
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.25rem 0.6rem;
  }
  .fx__ratio b {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .fx__ratio .pass {
    color: var(--color-success, #4a8);
  }
  .fx__ratio .fail {
    color: var(--color-error);
  }
  .fx .f + .f,
  .fx .f + .fs,
  .fx .fs__k + .f {
    margin-top: 0.875rem;
  }

  .lab__foot {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-base-300);
    padding-top: 1rem;
    max-width: 72ch;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: color-mix(in oklch, var(--color-base-content) 72%, transparent);
  }
  .lab__foot code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono, monospace);
    font-size: 0.8125em;
    background: var(--color-base-200);
    padding: 0.05em 0.3em;
    border-radius: 0.25rem;
  }

  /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     THE FOUR REGISTERS.

     `hq` HAS NO BLOCK AT ALL, and that is the point — it is `.f` as shipped,
     with this stylesheet silent. Anything that appears below is a PROPOSAL,
     which keeps the honest baseline honest.
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

  /* ── admin: `.admin-label` / `.admin-hint`, the third primitive ─────────── */
  [data-fx='admin'] .f__k {
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--text-whisper);
    margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
  }
  [data-fx='admin'] .f__h {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--text-whisper);
  }
  /* `.admin-label` never appears beside a `.f input` in the tree — it co-occurs
     with daisyUI's `input input-sm `, because it belongs to the
     half that was built on daisyUI. Reproduced here so this column is the real
     thing and not a straw man; it is meant to be dismissed on the LABEL, which
     is a fair fight only if everything else matches. */
  [data-fx='admin'] .f input,
  [data-fx='admin'] .f select,
  [data-fx='admin'] .f textarea {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    padding: 0.3125rem 0.75rem;
  }
  [data-fx='admin'] .f textarea {
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  /* ── corpus: what the quote and song sheets actually do ────────────────────
     `flex flex-col gap-1 text-sm` + `span.text-muted`, and daisyUI's
     `input input-sm ` rather than `.f`'s descendant selector.
     The required `<em>` is hidden because the corpus half HAS no word for
     required — FragmentSheet marks it with a red `*` carrying `aria-hidden`,
     which is why plan 38 · §2's finding F says the quote sheet's only required
     field is marked as nothing at all to a screen reader and on a phone. The
     asterisk is reproduced here so the comparison is fair rather than flattering. */
  [data-fx='corpus'] .f__k {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    letter-spacing: 0;
    text-transform: none;
    color: color-mix(in oklch, var(--color-base-content) 70%, transparent);
    margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
  }
  [data-fx='corpus'] .f__k em {
    font-size: 0;
    margin-left: 0.15rem;
  }
  [data-fx='corpus'] .f__k em::after {
    content: '*';
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-error);
  }
  [data-fx='corpus'] .f__h {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: color-mix(in oklch, var(--color-base-content) 45%, transparent);
  }
  [data-fx='corpus'] .f input,
  [data-fx='corpus'] .f select,
  [data-fx='corpus'] .f textarea {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    padding: 0.3125rem 0.75rem;
    border-radius: var(--radius-field, 0.5rem);
  }
  [data-fx='corpus'] .f textarea {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  /* ── ramp: `.f`'s geometry at plan 19's derived contrast floor ─────────────
     Dusk crosses 4.5:1 at 55% and paper at 65%, so 65% is the FLOOR that passes
     in both themes — which is the whole reason 19 pairs its steps by ratio
     rather than by the opacity they share. Nothing about the type moves: same
     10px, same uppercase, same tracking. If the only thing wrong with `.f` is
     that it is too faint, this column is the answer and the migration is
     unblocked.

     ⚠ THE HINT SAT AT 55% FOR ONE DRAFT AND STILL FAILED PAPER AT 3.42:1 —
     caught by the readout above, not by reasoning, because 55% is the DUSK
     threshold and I had reached for it out of the same sentence that names
     both. The correction has a consequence worth stating: once 65% is the
     floor, a hint CANNOT be quieter than a label by opacity, because there is
     no room left underneath. So the hierarchy has to be carried by size and
     case — which `.f` already does (10px uppercase key over 10px sentence
     hint) — and the ink stops being where that distinction lives. That is a
     real change to how this system expresses rank, and it is the strongest
     argument on this page for the third answer: keep `.f`'s geometry, take the
     corpus's ink. */
  [data-fx='ramp'] .f__k {
    color: color-mix(in oklch, var(--color-base-content) 80%, transparent);
  }
  [data-fx='ramp'] .f__h {
    color: color-mix(in oklch, var(--color-base-content) 65%, transparent);
  }
  [data-fx='ramp'] .fs__k {
    color: color-mix(in oklch, var(--cn) 72%, var(--color-base-content));
  }

  /* ── adminramp: THE PICK, at the floor ────────────────────────────────────
     Everything from `admin` above — 11.2px, .08em, daisyUI input chrome — with
     only the two opacities moved. Written as its own block rather than by
     stacking selectors so that deleting the `admin` column later does not
     silently take this one's geometry with it. */
  [data-fx='adminramp'] .f__k {
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
    color: color-mix(in oklch, var(--color-base-content) 80%, transparent);
  }
  [data-fx='adminramp'] .f__h {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: color-mix(in oklch, var(--color-base-content) 65%, transparent);
  }
  [data-fx='adminramp'] .f input,
  [data-fx='adminramp'] .f select,
  [data-fx='adminramp'] .f textarea {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    padding: 0.3125rem 0.75rem;
  }
  [data-fx='adminramp'] .f textarea {
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
</style>

<script>
  // The bench's controls. Written plainly and kept in this file — a lab that
  // reaches into `src/scripts/` leaves something behind when it is deleted.
  const lab = document.querySelector('.lab') as HTMLElement;

  function group(attr: string, value: string) {
    document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>(`[${attr}]`).forEach((b) => {
      const on = b.getAttribute(attr) === value;
      b.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(on));
    });
  }

  lab.dataset.view = 'grid';
  lab.dataset.w = 'wide';

  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-view]').forEach((b) =>
    b.addEventListener('click', () => {
      lab.dataset.view = b.dataset.view!;
      group('data-view', b.dataset.view!);
    }),
  );
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-w]').forEach((b) =>
    b.addEventListener('click', () => {
      lab.dataset.w = b.dataset.w!;
      group('data-w', b.dataset.w!);
    }),
  );

  /* ── THE MEASUREMENT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     ⚠ HALF OF THIS DECISION IS NOT A MATTER OF TASTE AND SHOULD NOT BE PUT TO
     THE EYE. Plan 19 derived the ink ramp by measuring: dusk crosses 4.5:1 at
     55% opacity and paper not until 65%, which is why its five steps are paired
     by RATIO rather than by the opacity they share. `.f__k` sits at 48% and
     `.f__h` at 42%, so the numbers below are the reason the `ramp` column
     exists at all.

     Computed live rather than quoted, because a quoted number goes stale the
     first time a token moves, and because these are `color-mix(… transparent)`
     values — the opacity is composited against whatever ground the card
     happens to have, so the ratio is not something you can read off the
     stylesheet. Same argument as measuring the header's x-coordinate in 16
     rather than asserting it didn't move. */
  /* ⚠ THE BROWSER DOES THE COLOUR CONVERSION, VIA A 1×1 CANVAS, AND THE FIRST
     VERSION OF THIS DID NOT — which is why it is worth the six extra lines.
     Every ink in this codebase is `color-mix(in oklch|oklab, …)`, so
     `getComputedStyle().color` hands back `oklab(0.9 0.02 0.01 / 0.48)`, not
     `rgb()`. Scraping numbers out of that string and feeding them to an sRGB
     luminance formula reports every register at ~1.03:1 — a number that says
     the text is invisible, on a page where you can plainly read it. It rendered
     and looked confident and was nonsense.

     Painting the ground and then the ink on top of it removes BOTH problems at
     once: the browser converts whatever colour space to sRGB, and the alpha
     composite happens in the pixel rather than in arithmetic I would have to
     get right. What comes back is the colour the eye actually receives. */
  const ctx = document.createElement('canvas').getContext('2d', { willReadFrequently: true })!;
  function paint(...layers: string[]): [number, number, number] {
    ctx.clearRect(0, 0, 1, 1);
    for (const c of layers) {
      ctx.fillStyle = c;
      ctx.fillRect(0, 0, 1, 1);
    }
    const d = ctx.getImageData(0, 0, 1, 1).data;
    return [d[0], d[1], d[2]];
  }
  /** sRGB → relative luminance (WCAG 2.x). */
  const lum = ([r, g, b]: number[]) =>
    [r, g, b]
      .map((v) => v / 255)
      .map((v) => (v <= 0.03928 ? v / 12.92 : ((v + 0.055) / 1.055) ** 2.4))
      .reduce((acc, v, i) => acc + v * [0.2126, 0.7152, 0.0722][i], 0);
  const ratio = (fg: string, bg: string) => {
    const [a, b] = [lum(paint(bg, fg)), lum(paint(bg))].sort((x, y) => y - x);
    return (a + 0.05) / (b + 0.05);
  };

  /** The ground an element actually sits on — walk up past transparent parents. */
  function ground(el: Element): string {
    let node: Element | null = el;
    while (node) {
      const c = getComputedStyle(node).backgroundColor;
      // Any non-zero alpha ends the walk. `rgba(0, 0, 0, 0)` is the only value
      // a browser reports for "no background", in every colour space.
      if (c && !/,\s*0\s*\)$/.test(c) && c !== 'transparent') return c;
      node = node.parentElement;
    }
    return getComputedStyle(document.body).backgroundColor;
  }

  function measure() {
    document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.fx').forEach((fx) => {
      const out = fx.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-ratio]');
      const label = fx.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.f__k');
      const hint = fx.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.f__h');
      if (!out || !label || !hint) return;
      const bg = ground(fx);
      const cell = (name: string, el: HTMLElement) => {
        const r = ratio(getComputedStyle(el).color, bg);
        // 4.5:1 is the Level AA floor for body-size text. Every label here is
        // under 18.66px, so none of them qualifies for the 3:1 large-text
        // exemption — which is itself part of the finding.
        const ok = r >= 4.5;
        return `<span class="${ok ? 'pass' : 'fail'}">${name} <b>${r.toFixed(2)}:1</b> ${ok ? '✓' : '✕'}</span>`;
      };
      out.innerHTML = cell('label', label) + cell('hint', hint);
    });
  }

  // Both themes, because half of plan 19's finding was that paper is dusk
  // inverted and the shared opacity ramp did not survive the trip. A register
  // judged in one theme is judged in half the building — and the ratios below
  // move when you flip, which is the single most useful thing on this page.
  document.querySelector('[data-theme-flip]')?.addEventListener('click', () => {
    const root = document.documentElement;
    root.dataset.theme = root.dataset.theme === 'paper' ? 'dusk' : 'paper';
    measure();
  });

  measure();

  // The segmented control is real markup, so make it behave — a dead control
  // reads as disabled and would score `.pseg__b` unfairly against a plain input.
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.pseg').forEach((seg) => {
    seg.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('.pseg__b').forEach((b) =>
      b.addEventListener('click', () => {
        seg.querySelectorAll('.pseg__b').forEach((o) => o.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(o === b)));
      }),
    );
  });
</script>
